Loop invariants are crucial to understanding loops so crucial that all but the most trivial loops should be documented with the invariants used to prove their annotations correct. In fact, (a first approximation to) the invariant should be developed before the loop is written and should act as a guide to the development of the loop. For example, since the fourth point for proving correctness of the loop is $P \land \neg B \Rightarrow R$ , given $P$ and $R$ one can derive the loop guard by solving this expression for $B$ .

